    "body": "Interesting. /cc @josepharhar.\r\n\r\nOn the one hand, interop is good, and if both implementers of dialog are interested in doing this, I'd be hesitant to put on the brakes.\r\n\r\nOn the other hand, I feel pretty uncomfortable about doing this, given that we don't do this for any other keyboard-triggered UI in the platform. For example, we don't specify the sequences of events that occur when using <kbd>Esc</kbd> to get out of fullscreen, or the sequence of events that occur when using <kbd>Alt+←</kbd> to go back, or the sequence of events that occur when using <kbd>Ctrl+F4</kbd> to close a browsing context.\r\n\r\nI think one midpoint that'd be worth doing for sure would be writing [`.optional` tests](https://web-platform-tests.org/writing-tests/file-names.html) to help with interop.\r\n\r\nAfter that, I'm not so sure. I'm especially torn about giving guidance on what happens if the keydown event is canceled. It's interesting, and a bit unexpected, that that impacts things. (I imagine it doesn't for the other examples I mentioned.) So if that's a behavior we want to keep, then mentioning it in the spec might be a good idea, at least in some form.",
